/// known-gap: interp has no event log (the interpreter is being retired)
/// `:event(who, what, whom, telling)` writes a line of narration to the
/// event log and changes nothing: `tick N | who | what | whom | "telling"`.
/// Who is an object or a text; whom an object, a text, a list or nil; the
/// telling a text, or a map of tellings to weights, one drawn by weight
/// (0 can't be drawn; all 0 writes nothing and gives nil). It gives back
/// the telling. Its draws have their own random stream, so the program's
/// own draws are the same with or without events.
<>Realm |
    name: ..
    wars: 0
xx
<>Person |
    name: ..
    age: 0
xx
:rand_seed(7)
r <> Realm | name: "Arveth", wars: 1
k <> Person | name: "Toren", age: 70
:event_log("des_event.log.txt")
:tick()
t | :event(r, "ruler died", k, {"fell in battle": r >> wars * .5, "of the plague": 0.0, "of old age": k >> age / 80.0})
:say(:has(["fell in battle", "of old age"], t))
:event(r, "succeeded by", k, "the eldest son")
:event(r, "omen", nil, "a comet over the capital")
:event("the world", "long winter")
:event(r, "treaty", [k, r])
:say(:event(r, "nothing fits", k, {"a": 0, "b": 0.0}).nix?)
a | pick 1 from 0...1000
:event_log(nil)
:rand_seed(7)
b | pick 1 from 0...1000
:say(a == b)
lines | :lines(:read_text("des_event.log.txt"))
:say(:len(lines))
repeat :range(1, :len(lines)) as i
    :say(lines[i])
xx
:delete_path!("des_event.log.txt")
attempt
    :event(r, "bad", k, {"a": -1})
rescue e
    :say(:has("{e}", "R1253"))
xx
attempt
    :event(r, 3)
rescue e
    :say(:has("{e}", "R1253"))
xx
